  | s, c :: cs => if (blockFilter c).foldl step s = c.2.2.2 then runChecked c.2.2.2 cs else none

/-- One block at a time: if block `c` checks from state `s`, the rest of the list is checked from
`c`'s end state. The certificate proves each block in its own theorem and chains them with this
lemma, because the kernel frees its working memory after every theorem: a 40-block `decide +kernel`
needs about 10 GB, a single block about 0.6 GB. -/
